@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "DevOps vs Traditional IT";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">Understanding the differences between modern DevOps practices and traditional IT methodologies is crucial for organizations aiming to improve efficiency, collaboration, and innovation.</p>
    <hr />

    <!-- What is DevOps? -->
    <div class="mt-4">
        <h2>What is DevOps?</h2>
        <p>
            DevOps is a <strong>culture, set of practices, and tools</strong> that integrates <strong>software development (Dev)</strong> and <strong>IT operations (Ops)</strong>.
            It <strong>automates and enhances</strong> collaboration between teams to ensure <strong>faster, more reliable</strong> software delivery. DevOps emphasizes <strong>continuous integration (CI)</strong>, <strong>continuous delivery (CD)</strong>, and <strong>infrastructure as code (IaC)</strong> to streamline workflows and reduce manual intervention.
        </p>
        <p>
            The core principles of DevOps include:
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Collaboration:</strong> Breaking down silos between development and operations teams.</li>
                <li><strong>Automation:</strong> Automating repetitive tasks like testing, deployment, and monitoring.</li>
                <li><strong>Continuous Improvement:</strong> Regularly refining processes based on feedback and metrics.</li>
                <li><strong>Customer-Centric Approach:</strong> Delivering value to customers faster and more reliably.</li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="~/css/images/AzureDevOps/devops-concept.png" 
                 alt="DevOps Concept" 
                 class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>DevOps enables continuous collaboration and automation.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- What is Traditional IT? -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What is Traditional IT?</h2>
        <p>
            Traditional IT follows a <strong>waterfall-style</strong>, sequential approach where development, testing, and deployment occur in <strong>separate phases</strong>.
            While structured, this method often leads to <strong>delays, inefficiencies, and communication gaps</strong>. Traditional IT relies heavily on manual processes, which can result in slower release cycles and a higher risk of errors.
        </p>
        <p>
            Key characteristics of Traditional IT include:
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Phased Approach:</strong> Development, testing, and deployment are handled in distinct stages.</li>
                <li><strong>Manual Processes:</strong> Limited automation, leading to slower and error-prone workflows.</li>
                <li><strong>Siloed Teams:</strong> Development and operations teams work independently with minimal collaboration.</li>
                <li><strong>Rigid Structure:</strong> Changes are difficult to implement once a phase is completed.</li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        
    </div>

    <!-- Key Differences Table -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Key Differences: DevOps vs Traditional IT</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">The table below highlights the fundamental differences between DevOps and Traditional IT methodologies:</p>
        <table class="table table-bordered text-center mt-3">
            <thead class="table-dark">
                <tr>
                    <th>Aspect</th>
                    <th>DevOps</th>
                    <th>Traditional IT</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Process</strong></td>
                    <td>Continuous Integration & Continuous Deployment (CI/CD)</td>
                    <td>Sequential phases (Dev → Test → Deploy)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Collaboration</strong></td>
                    <td>Developers and operations teams work together</td>
                    <td>Teams work in silos</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Speed</strong></td>
                    <td>Faster releases with automation</td>
                    <td>Slower releases due to manual processes</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Tools</strong></td>
                    <td>Automation with Jenkins, Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform</td>
                    <td>Traditional server and deployment tools</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Feedback Loop</strong></td>
                    <td>Real-time feedback and continuous improvement</td>
                    <td>Delayed feedback due to phased approach</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Risk Management</strong></td>
                    <td>Early detection of issues through automated testing</td>
                    <td>Higher risk of errors due to manual testing</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Conclusion -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            DevOps fosters <strong>collaboration, automation, and agility</strong>, enabling <strong>faster</strong> and <strong>more efficient</strong> software releases.
            In contrast, <strong>Traditional IT</strong>, though structured, often <strong>hinders speed</strong> due to <strong>manual processes</strong> and <strong>separate teams</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Today, <strong>most organizations</strong> are transitioning to DevOps for <strong>improved efficiency, reliability, and innovation</strong>.
            By embracing DevOps practices, businesses can stay competitive in an ever-evolving technological landscape.
        </p>
        
    </div>
</div>
